      "id": 1,
      "text": "Dekorra was named after a respected Ho-Chunk leader named Decorah.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dekorra%2C_Wisconsin"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Native American peoples, including the Ho-Chunk, originally inhabited the region encompassing present-day Dekorra.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/dekorra_wisconsin"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Several major Native American trails once converged near the old village of Dekorra, creating a strategic meeting point.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12,
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/dekorra_wisconsin"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Immigrants from France, Scotland, England, Germany, and Norway worked the land that became the Town of Dekorra in 1848.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://dekorra-wi.gov/history-of-dekorra/"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Inch Methodist Church and Dekorra Lutheran Church represent the area's early religious and social organization from the mid-1800s.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4,
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
